course: dictionary
lesson: 12515
title: Words derived from other words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Derived means coming from something else, or made from something else. Many English words are derived from older words in Latin, Greek or French. «The word helpful is derived from the word help.» Help came first, and helpful was made from it by adding -ful.
explain: You can spot derived words by their parts. A small piece added to the start or end of a root word makes a new derived word. «Happy» gives «unhappy» and «happiness», and both come from happy. When you know the root word, the new word is much easier to understand.
explain: Maths and science use derived in a different way. «Speed is a derived unit, worked out by dividing distance by time.» A derived unit is found from other facts. When you see the phrase derived from, look for the source it came from.
fact: Derived means coming from or made from something else.
fact: «Derived» is the past form of the verb derive.
fact: «Derived from» is a common phrase that tells you where something came from.
fact: A derived word is made from a root word, often with a prefix or a suffix added.
fact: In maths, a derived formula or unit is worked out from other facts or rules.
exercise: «The word helpful is derived from the word help.» What does derived mean here? | Written in capital letters | Shouted loudly | *Came from or was made from help | Forgotten by everyone
exercise: Which word is derived from «happy»? | Sunny | Running | Table | *Happiness
exercise: In maths, a derived formula is one that is | drawn in colour | the first formula ever written | *worked out from other facts | too hard to read
test: «Speed is a derived unit: it is worked out from distance and time.» What does derived mean in this sentence? | Given a new colour | Lost and never found | Measured with a ruler only | *Worked out from other facts
test: Which phrase shows where a word came from? | derived to | derived about | derived in | *derived from
exam: «Derive» is a verb. Which word is the past form in «She derived pleasure from reading»? | deriving | *derived | derives | derive
